  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a lung-centered model for the development of autoimmunity, likely in rheumatoid arthritis. It links environmental exposure and genetic susceptibility to the production of ACPA antibodies before disease onset.\n\nOn the left, inhaled factors such as smoke, silica, and other irritants appear to act on lung tissue and bronchoalveolar lavage proteins, causing lung changes. These proteins then become modified by citrullination, shown by small added markers on the protein chains. The implication is that the lung is a site where normal proteins are altered into potentially immunogenic citrullinated proteins.\n\nOn the right, susceptibility genes are shown above antigen-presenting cells (APC), suggesting that genetic background shapes how these altered proteins are recognized by the immune system. APC activation leads to activated T cells, which then stimulate B cells. This culminates in production of ACPA, illustrated as antibodies increasing over time.\n\nThe small graph at the bottom right indicates that ACPA levels rise as the time to disease onset shortens. Overall, the image summarizes a pathway in which environmental lung injury and genetic risk cooperate to generate citrullinated antigens, trigger adaptive immune responses, and progressively increase autoantibody levels before clinical disease appears.",
